{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1781777281,"nonce":"nGJvC4tzyoCce9LfW0TktjRJJU4=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABGpOY8A","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/163#issuecomment-4740847856"},"message":"## Field data point (2026-06-17, deployment #295): connector replaced, intermittency persists → confirms root cause is NOT the connector\n\nThe SVS sound-speed feed was **intermittent again** on 2026-06-17 (recovered ~14:30 for ~25 Hz briefly, dropped ~14:33; `sound_speed_bridge` diagnostic \"No reading for ~355 s\"). Same cut-in/cut-out pattern as 2026-06-16.\n\n**Key:** the operator **replaced the SVS connector** on 06-17 and **the intermittency persisted**. That **disproves the \"loose connector\" hypothesis** that had been carried forward — and is consistent with **this issue (all-NUL bytes on `/dev/ttyS0`)**: the fault is on the **sensor / serial side (probe output), not the cabling/connector**. Power-cycle-immune, matches the all-NUL symptom.\n\nImpact was benign operationally: sidescan unaffected (1500 m/s fallback, decoupled); M3 held last-good SV between gaps. `temp_sound_speed.py` (marine_tools #53 territory) remains the steady-feed stopgap. Recommend treating the SVS hardware/serial root-cause here as the live blocker for clean SV.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}